# Setting up Gemini for the First Time

2026-09-06

## Preamble

I enjoy RSS for its simplicity and I don't really use social media. I follow Ploum, and after reading his article about Offpunk Culture I found his Offpunk Browser and started playing with it. What a relaxing world! I'm already one to pay with cash, code in Common Lisp for personal projects, and avoid LLM technology entirely, so this place fits my preferences. Already on Day 3 I've found myself setting up this little server. It's 08:00 on Sunday and I have nothing scheduled until 18:00. Why not?

=> gemini://ploum.net/index_en.gmi Ploum's Blog (English)
=> gemini://offpunk.net/ Offpunk Browser

## The Setup

### The Server

A quick search revealed that Agate is a decent choice for simply serving files. And since Gemini is so simple to begin with, is much else required?

=> gemini://qwertqwefsday.eu/agate.gmi Agate (Rust-based Gemini file server)

I installed Agate from the Arch Linux AUR to ensure it's tracked by my system's package manager. That also takes care of minor setup details otherwise not covered by a raw `cargo install` invocation.

### Writing pages with Gemtext

The Gemtext markup format is also fine, no real surprises other than the expectation that all paragraphs are written on a single line. That's a hindrance to users of anything Emacs or Vim-like (Emacs for myself), since while my editor does do visual line wrapping automatically, I can no longer easily move between what appear to be individual lines to make corrections. Oh well.

I considered for a moment installing an Emacs major mode for Gemtext, but it's really not needed. Manually switching to `text-mode` seems fine.

=> gemini://geminiprotocol.net/docs/gemtext.gmi Official Gemtext Introduction

### Deployment

I will commit these files once in order to have something to serve, try some things, then return here. Just a moment, please.

I'm trying to get Agate running on the same physical server as my normal website. Steps:

* Update the machine.
* Fix broken packages.
* Install Agate.
* Pull these files.
* Run the server.
* Connect from Offpunk.

I installed the `agate-bin` package in order to avoid problems with building packages as the root user.

Running Agate on my remote server with:

```
agate --content gemini/ --addr 0.0.0.0:1965 --hostname www.fosskers.ca --lang en-US
```

was successful. I can connect to it from Offpunk with:

```
go gemini://www.fosskers.ca
```

Since this is running on a different port than the normal webserver, there was no need to specify a subdomain or go into my Domain's DNS settings. They don't appear to be fighting over the `0.0.0.0` IP.

Now, updating the files should just be a matter of committing changes, and pulling them on the server's end. The Agate server need not be restarted.

A success! And all before breakfast. As we say in Japanese, that was 朝飯前. Now how do I get the various Gemini aggregators to find this Capsule?

## Non-goals

I've decided that for the moment there's no point in trying to unify my current website with this Capsule, or to worry about converting the OrgMode-based blog posts into Gemtext. Perhaps later when I redo my website in general, but not now. For now, simply using Agate to serve these files in sufficient.

